  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 740 PM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Mainly dry with seasonable temperatures and \nsignificantly lower humidity through Wednesday. Temperatures \nwarm back above normal late in the week as an upper ridge \nbuilds back into the region.\n\nThe latest surface analysis depicted a large (~1025mb) area of\nhigh pressure centered across the eastern Great Lakes this\nevening. The high will gradually spread eastward overnight. Temperatures\nas of 730 PM ranged from the lower 70s N to the upper 70s S \nwith dew points in the 50s across the northern two thirds of the\nFA and 60s across far S VA/NE NC. As the high continues to build\nin overnight, winds will start to diminish on land and skies \nwill remain mostly clear. With light winds and mostly clear \nskies, this will be a favorable environment for radiational \ncooling. Dew points will drop into the 50s for most of the \nforecast area, so lows will likely be able to drop in the mid to\nupper 50s for most locations. Northern portions of the FA could\neven see lower 50s with localized upper 40s not out of the \nquestion in Louisa County. Near the coast, temperatures will \nlikely remain in the mid 60s.\n\nDew points are forecast to remain very comfortable through at \nleast Thursday as strong sfc high pressure (~1030 mb), sets up \nfrom off the New England coast SW into the Carolina coastal \nplain through midweek. Highs will stay mostly in the low-mid 80s\nthrough Wed with lows in the 50s to lower 60s (locally warmer \nat the immediate coast). The upper level ridge is forecast to \nexpand E-NE to the TN Valley and central Appalachians Thu-Fri.\nThis will allow temperatures to climb back to above normal for \nmid- September, with highs generally in the mid-upper 80s. Dry\nweather likely persists through Saturday. However, cannot rule\nout a few isolated showers or storms across the northern\nportions of the FA on Friday with a cold front (15-20% PoPs).",
